# Line-ending policy: LF everywhere — in the repository AND in the working tree.
#
# Without this file, a Windows checkout (core.autocrlf=true) gets a CRLF
# working tree while the repository itself stores LF. Every generator in this
# repo writes LF, so the two disagree the moment one of them runs:
# `cargo test` rewrites all 79 JSON files under
# smart-contracts/astrea/contracts/event-escrow/test_snapshots/ and `git status`
# then lists every one of them as modified — even though `git diff` is empty
# and the blob hash still matches the index. What went stale is the cached stat
# size, not the content. The danger is a `git add -A` that sweeps all 79 into a
# PR and buries the real change.
#
# graphify-out/ sits on the same trap from the other side: the drift check in
# .github/workflows/ci.yml and the pre-push hook both diff tool output that CI
# regenerates on ubuntu-latest, where it is always LF.
#
# `eol=lf` — not `text=auto` on its own — is the half that actually fixes this.
# `text=auto` normalizes what gets committed but still hands Windows a CRLF
# working tree, which is precisely where the mismatch is created.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Files that break outright with CRLF. Marked `text` (unconditional) rather
# than `text=auto` so they normalize even if content sniffing misjudges them.
# The husky hooks and the contract Makefile all run under sh on Windows, where
# a trailing CR is parsed as part of the command and the failure is opaque.
*.sh     text eol=lf
Makefile text eol=lf
.husky/* text eol=lf

# The one exception to the rule: cmd.exe genuinely requires CRLF. None are
# tracked today — this is here so the first one added doesn't silently break.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Binary — never normalize, never diff as text. `text=auto` already detects
# these by content; declaring them is cheaper than trusting the sniffer and
# states the intent. *.wasm is the deployable contract artifact (built, not
# committed today) and must never be touched by a text filter.
*.png  binary
*.ico  binary
*.wasm binary
